window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title": "Computer Running Slow? 7 Free Fixes to Try Today", "description": "Slow Windows PC? These 7 free fixes, from startup programs to disk cleanup and system file repair, can speed it up without buying anything.", "category": "Windows & PC", "url": "articles/computer-running-slow-fixes.html", "date": "2026-10-15", "text": "A slow computer is frustrating, but it rarely means the machine is dead. Most slowdowns come from too many programs starting with Windows, a nearly full drive, or background tasks using all the memory. Try these fixes in order. They are free and safe if you follow the steps. Fix 1: Restart the computer It sounds too simple, but a restart clears memory and stops stuck background processes. If you usually use sleep or hibernate, your PC may have been running for weeks. Do a full Restart (not Shut down, which can use fast startup). Fix 2: Find what is using your resources Press Ctrl + Shift + Esc to open Task Manager. Click the Processes tab. Click the CPU , Memory and Disk headings to sort by usage. If one program is using most of the CPU, memory or disk, close it. Right-click it and choose End task . Do not end tasks you do not recognize, especially those named after Windows, until you check what they are. Fix 3: Turn off startup programs Too many apps launching at startup is a top cause of slow boots. In Task Manager, click the Startup apps tab. Sort by Startup impact . Right-click apps you do not need immediately (chat apps, updaters, music players) and choose Disable . This does not uninstall anything. You can enable them again later. Fix 4: Free up disk space Windows slows down when the system drive is nearly full. Try to keep at least 15 to 20 percent free . Open Settings > System > Storage . Turn on Storage Sense or click Temporary files and delete what you do not need (such as temporary files and the Recycle Bin). Click Apps (or Installed apps ) and uninstall programs you no longer use. Move large photos and videos to an external drive or cloud storage. Fix 5: Scan for malware Malware and unwanted programs can slow everything down. Open Windows Security from the Start menu. Choose Virus & threat protection > Scan options . Select Full scan and click Scan now . A full scan can take an hour or more, so run it when you do not need the computer. Fix 6: Update Windows and drivers Open Settings > Windows Update and install all available updates, then restart. For graphics and chipset drivers, download updates from your computer maker's support page or from the graphics card maker's site. Outdated drivers can cause freezes and slow performance. Fix 7: Repair damaged system files Corrupted Windows files can cause slowness and strange errors. Click Start, type cmd , right-click Command Prompt , and choose Run as administrator . Run: sfc /scannow When it finishes, run: DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth Restart the computer. Each command can take several minutes. Let them finish without closing the window. If it is still slow: hardware upgrades When software fixes are not enough, hardware is often the limit: Upgrade When it helps SSD instead of a hard drive The biggest speed boost for an older PC that still has a mechanical hard drive. Boot and loading times drop sharply More RAM If Task Manager shows memory nearly full while you work (8 GB i"}, {"title": "Epson Printer Says \"Paper Out\" but Paper Is Loaded? Try This Fix First", "description": "Your Epson printer shows a Paper Out error even though paper is loaded. Here are the causes and 7 fixes, from the quickest to the most thorough.", "category": "Printers", "url": "articles/epson-printer-paper-out-error.html", "date": "2026-10-15", "text": "You loaded a fresh stack of paper, pressed print, and the printer still says \"Paper Out\" . It is one of the most common printer complaints, and in most cases you can fix it in a few minutes without paying for a repair. The steps below work for most Epson inkjet and EcoTank printers. Menus and part names differ slightly between models, so check your printer's manual if something looks different. Why does the printer say \"Paper Out\" when there is paper? The printer decides whether paper is present using a sensor and a pick-up roller. The error usually appears when: The paper is not sitting flat or straight in the tray The edge guides are too loose or too tight A small piece of paper, a label or dust is blocking the paper path The feed roller is dirty or worn and cannot grip the sheet The paper size or paper source in the print settings does not match what is loaded The printer software has a stuck job or a glitch Start with fix 1 and work down the list. Stop as soon as the printer works. Fix 1: Turn the printer off and back on A power cycle clears many temporary errors. Turn the printer off with its power button. Unplug the power cable from the wall or the printer and wait about 60 seconds. Plug it back in and turn it on. Try printing again. Fix 2: Reload the paper properly Bad loading is the most common cause, so it is worth doing carefully. Remove all the paper from the tray. Fan the stack and tap it on a flat surface so the edges line up. Never mix different sizes or thicknesses. Load the stack so it sits flat against the side of the tray with the printable side facing the way your model requires (usually up for rear trays). Slide the edge guide until it touches the paper snugly. It should not bend the paper. Do not overfill. Keep the stack below the limit mark. Fold out any paper support or extension if your printer has one. Do not use curled, damp, very thin or already-printed-on paper. These are common reasons for pick-up problems. Fix 3: Check for paper scraps and debris Even a small torn corner can block the sensor. Turn the printer off and unplug it. Remove the paper and look into the paper feed area with a flashlight. If your model has a rear cover, open it and check the path. Remove any scraps, labels, clips or dust. Use your fingers or tweezers gently. Never use sharp tools and never force anything. Put the covers back and try again. Fix 4: Clean the paper feed roller If the roller is dirty, it slides over the paper instead of pulling it in. With the printer unplugged, find the rubber feed roller that touches the top sheet. Wipe it gently with a lint-free cloth lightly dampened with plain water. Let it dry completely before you reload paper and power on. Epson publishes an official cleaning procedure for each model. If you have the manual, follow that version. Fix 5: Make sure the paper settings match A mismatch between the software and the real paper can trigger the error. Open the document, choose Print , then open Printer Properties "}, {"title": "Excel Inventory Sheet: Track Stock In and Out With Simple Formulas", "description": "Build an Excel stock monitoring sheet that updates automatically. Uses SUMIFS and IF formulas that work in Excel 2019 and newer, with a low-stock warning.", "category": "Excel Tutorials", "url": "articles/excel-inventory-stock-monitoring.html", "date": "2026-10-15", "text": "You do not need special software to monitor stock. With three sheets and two formulas, Excel can show your current stock , mark items that need reordering, and update automatically every time you record an entry. This guide uses SUMIFS and IF , which work in Excel 2010 and newer, including Excel 2019. What you will build Three sheets in one workbook: Inventory : one row per item, with the current stock calculated for you Stock In : a log of everything received Stock Out : a log of everything sold or used Step 1: Create the Inventory sheet Rename a sheet to Inventory and set up these columns in row 1: A B C D E F G Item Code Item Name Reorder Level Total In Total Out Current Stock Status Fill in rows 2 and below with your item codes and names, and a reorder level for each (the number at which you want a warning). For example: Item Code Item Name Reorder Level A001 A4 Paper Ream 10 A002 Black Ink Bottle 5 A003 USB Cable 8 Step 2: Create the Stock In and Stock Out sheets Create two sheets named Stock In and Stock Out . Use the same columns in both: A B C Date Item Code Quantity Every time you receive stock, add a new row in Stock In . Every time you sell or use stock, add a row in Stock Out . If you already have stock on hand, enter it as your first Stock In entry, dated the day you start. Step 3: Add the formulas on the Inventory sheet Click cell D2 on the Inventory sheet and enter: =SUMIFS('Stock In'!$C:$C,'Stock In'!$B:$B,$A2) This adds up all quantities in Stock In for the item code in column A. Click E2 and enter: =SUMIFS('Stock Out'!$C:$C,'Stock Out'!$B:$B,$A2) Click F2 and enter: =D2-E2 Click G2 and enter: =IF(F2<=0,\"OUT OF STOCK\",IF(F2<=C2,\"REORDER\",\"OK\")) Select D2:G2 and drag the fill handle (the small square in the corner) down to copy the formulas to all your items. Step 4: Add a dropdown for item codes This prevents typing mistakes that would break your totals. Go to the Stock In sheet and select column B (from B2 downward). Click Data > Data Validation . Under Allow , choose List . In Source , enter =Inventory!$A$2:$A$200 . Click OK. Repeat for the Stock Out sheet. Now each entry uses a code from your Inventory list. Step 5: Highlight low stock automatically On the Inventory sheet, select the G column data (G2 downward). Click Home > Conditional Formatting > Highlight Cells Rules > Text that Contains . Type REORDER and choose a yellow fill. Click OK. Repeat for OUT OF STOCK with a red fill and OK with a green fill. Step 6: Show the item name automatically on the log sheets (optional) On the Stock In sheet, add a column D with the heading Item Name . In D2 enter: =IFERROR(VLOOKUP(B2,Inventory!$A:$B,2,FALSE),\"\") Copy it down. The name now appears when you pick a code. Use VLOOKUP rather than XLOOKUP if you use Excel 2019, because XLOOKUP is only available in newer versions (Excel 2021 and Microsoft 365). Step 7: Add the stock value (optional) Add a Unit Cost column in Inventory (for example, column H) and a Stock Value column (column I) "}, {"title": "LAN Connected but No Internet? Step-by-Step Troubleshooting", "description": "Your computer shows it is connected to the network but websites will not open. Follow these steps to find whether the problem is the PC, router, DNS or provider.", "category": "Networking", "url": "articles/lan-connected-no-internet.html", "date": "2026-10-15", "text": "The network icon says you are connected, but websites will not load. This usually means your computer is talking to the router, but the router (or something after it) is not passing traffic to the internet. The fastest way to fix it is to find where the break is. This guide helps you do that in a few minutes. Step 1: Find out how big the problem is Ask yourself: Do other devices (phones, other PCs) have internet on the same network? Does it fail on every website or only some? Did it stop suddenly, or after a Windows update, a power cut or a router change? Result Likely cause Only one computer has no internet Problem is on that computer (settings, adapter, driver) All devices have no internet Router, modem or the internet provider Some sites work, some do not DNS problem Step 2: Restart the modem and router Many problems end here. Unplug the power from the modem and the router. Wait 30 seconds. Plug in the modem first and wait until its lights settle, then plug in the router. Wait two minutes and test again. Step 3: Check cables and lights Make sure the network cable clicks firmly into both the computer and the router Look at the lights on the network port. A light that is off can point to a bad cable or port Try a different cable and a different port on the router Check the cable running into the modem is not loose Step 4: Run the Windows network troubleshooter Open Settings > Network & Internet . Choose Network troubleshooter (or Advanced network settings > Network reset later if needed). Follow the steps and read the result it gives. Step 5: Release and renew the IP address Click Start, type cmd , right-click Command Prompt , and choose Run as administrator . Type these commands, pressing Enter after each one: ipconfig /release ipconfig /renew ipconfig /flushdns Test the internet again. Step 6: Check what IP address you received In the same Command Prompt window, type: ipconfig Look at IPv4 Address : If it starts with 169.254 , your computer did not get an address from the router. Check the cable and router, and make sure DHCP is turned on in the router settings. If it looks like 192.168.x.x or 10.x.x.x , you have a normal address, so continue. Step 7: Find where the connection stops using ping Type these commands one at a time: ping 192.168.1.1 (use your router's address, shown as Default Gateway in ipconfig ) ping 8.8.8.8 ping google.com What happens Meaning First ping fails Problem between your PC and the router (cable, adapter, IP settings) First works, second fails Router has no internet (modem, provider) Second works, third fails DNS problem Step 8: Fix a DNS problem If ping 8.8.8.8 works but ping google.com fails, change your DNS servers: Open Control Panel > Network and Sharing Center > Change adapter settings . Right-click your connection and choose Properties . Double-click Internet Protocol Version 4 (TCP/IPv4) . Choose Use the following DNS server addresses and enter 8.8.8.8 and 8.8.4.4 (or 1.1.1.1 and 1.0.0.1 ). Click OK and test. "}, {"title": "PC Turns On but No Display? 5 Fixes to Try Before Calling a Technician", "description": "Fans spinning but the screen stays black? Follow these 5 checks, from cables to RAM, to find out why your computer shows no display.", "category": "Windows & PC", "url": "articles/pc-turns-on-no-display.html", "date": "2026-10-15", "text": "You press the power button, the fans spin and the lights turn on, but the screen stays black. This is a very common problem, and the cause is often something simple like a loose cable or a memory stick that has shifted. Work through the checks below in order. Always turn the computer off and unplug it before touching anything inside the case. Quick checks first (2 minutes) Before you open anything: Make sure the monitor is powered on and the power light is lit Check the monitor's input source (HDMI, DisplayPort, VGA, DVI) matches the cable you use Press a key and move the mouse in case the PC is in sleep mode Look for any beeps or blinking lights on the case, which can point to the problem Fix 1: Check and reseat the video cable Turn everything off. Unplug the video cable at both ends and plug it back in firmly. Try a different cable if you have one. Cables fail more often than people expect. If your monitor has another input, try that one. Test with a different monitor or a TV if possible. This tells you whether the monitor or the computer is at fault. Fix 2: Use the correct video port If your desktop has a dedicated graphics card (a card with its own fan, installed in a slot at the back), the cable must go into the graphics card , not the motherboard's built-in port. A cable plugged into the wrong port gives a black screen even though the PC is running. If you do not have a graphics card, use the motherboard's video port. Fix 3: Disconnect extra devices Sometimes a faulty USB device or a second screen stops the PC from starting properly. Turn off the PC and unplug everything except the power, the monitor and the keyboard. Turn it on and see if the display appears. If it does, plug the other devices back in one at a time to find the faulty one. Fix 4: Reseat the RAM (memory) Loose memory is one of the top causes of a no-display problem. Turn off the PC, unplug the power cable and press the power button once to drain leftover power. Open the side panel. Touch a metal part of the case to discharge static electricity. Press down the clips on each side of a memory stick, remove it and push it back until the clips click. If you have more than one stick, try one stick at a time in the first slot. A faulty stick can be the entire problem. Close the case, reconnect the power and turn on. Fix 5: Reset the BIOS (clear CMOS) A wrong BIOS setting after a power cut or a hardware change can stop the display. Turn off and unplug the PC. Find the round, silver coin battery on the motherboard. Remove it, wait about 5 minutes, then put it back in the same direction. Plug in the power and start the PC. The BIOS will use its default settings. Many motherboards also have a Clear CMOS jumper or button. Check the motherboard manual for the exact location. For laptops Hold the power button for about 30 seconds with the charger unplugged to do a hard reset, then try again Remove the battery if it is removable, hold the power button for 30 seconds, then reconnect everyth"}];